@@ -0,0 +1,33 @@
+The homepage loads `performance-monitor.js` with no dependencies. It observes
+`interaction-contentful-paint` for the navigation bar, Order Now, and Seasonal
+Favorites using stable `data-performance-area` labels. Event Timing supplies
+interaction IDs for attribution; captured trusted input timestamps cover fast
+handlers below Event Timing's minimum reporting threshold when timestamps match.
+If neither attribution is available, the interaction is left unlabeled and is
+not reported as a control metric.
+
+Inspect the latest 100 records in `window.dailyGrindPerformance.records`, or
+subscribe to `window.addEventListener('daily-grind:performance', event => ...)`
+and consume `event.detail`. No analytics endpoint is configured. Records contain
+milliseconds from the initiating interaction to content presentation, rather than
+time since page load. Each progressively larger paint produces a candidate update;
+these are not finalized Core Web Vitals scores. Consumers should keep the latest
+candidate for each interaction ID and metric type.
+
+Soft-navigation records contain `fcpDuration`, `lcpDuration`, `interactionId`,
+`navigationId`, and destination `path`. Paints are joined by interaction ID so
+content painted before a URL update is included. Query strings and DOM nodes are
+not included in reports. Buffers are bounded to 100 interactions and records.
+
+These browser APIs have limited availability. Check
+`window.dailyGrindPerformance.supported` for support; unsupported APIs are skipped
+without substituting click duration or animation-frame estimates. No contentful
+paint means no measurement. The current site's ordinary links perform full
+document navigations, so they do not produce soft-navigation records or measure
+destination paints in the old document. The monitor will observe client-side view
+navigations if the application introduces them; it does not change routing.
+
+Reference: https://developer.chrome.com/docs/web-platform/soft-navigations
+
+Run the isolated observer tests with `node --test performance-monitor.test.cjs`.
+They use synthetic performance entries and do not start a server or browser.
